  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual landscape-to-ocean cross section linking climate, erosion, rock type, and long-term carbon fluxes. A river network drains from cooler, high mountains into lower, hotter and/or wetter terrain and eventually to the sea, while different bedrock types are labeled as sedimentary rocks on the continental side and volcanic rocks closer to the margin and island arc. Stars mark example sites spanning low to high erosion, and the black dashed line appears to indicate a climatic or topographic transition across the catchment.\n\nInset bar charts summarize how carbon transfers vary among these settings. They compare inorganic and organic contributions, including silicate weathering, carbonate-sulfide weathering, burial of organic carbon, and oxidation of petrogenic organic carbon, with arrows showing whether the net effect is a CO2 sink or source. Across the transect, the balance shifts depending on erosion rate and lithology: some settings are shown as net CO2 sinks, while others tend toward sources or weaker sinks. The overall message is that Earth-surface carbon cycling is not controlled by climate alone, but by the interaction of climate, topography, erosion intensity, and the kind of rocks being weathered, with material ultimately transported toward the subduction zone.",
